  "Number": 2,
  "Question": "Which of the following is equal to \\[1 + \\dfrac{1}{1 + \\dfrac{1}{1 + 1}}?\\]",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{4}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{3}\\)",
    "D": "\\(2\\)",
    "E": "\\(3\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Each fraction bar groups its entire denominator, so begin with the innermost sum, \\(1+1=2\\). The denominator of the outer fraction is then\n\\[\n1+\\frac12=\\frac32.\n\\]\nDividing \\(1\\) by \\(\\frac32\\) gives its reciprocal, \\(\\frac23\\). Therefore the expression equals\n\\[\n1+\\frac23=\\frac53.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\frac53\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
